1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,230
At the Corner Cafe, Asha's new weather
script says a package is missing.

2
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:07,730
She asks an AI assistant what to do.

3
00:00:07,767 --> 00:00:09,848
Read the package name slowly.

4
00:00:09,868 --> 00:00:11,080
Would you run it?

5
00:00:11,687 --> 00:00:13,335
Welcome to Chapter 24.

6
00:00:13,355 --> 00:00:18,947
Today Asha gives her project its own
Python, installs packages safely, and

7
00:00:18,967 --> 00:00:20,767
checks every name first.

8
00:00:21,387 --> 00:00:25,358
Here is Asha's draft list of packages for
the weather board.

9
00:00:25,378 --> 00:00:30,082
By the end, her own program will check it
before anything is installed.

10
00:00:30,683 --> 00:00:35,433
Create and activate a virtual environment,
and install a package into it.

11
00:00:35,467 --> 00:00:40,569
Record exact versions, and check a package
name before you install it.

12
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:46,275
Asha needs requests for live weather, and
tabulate for a neat table.

13
00:00:46,295 --> 00:00:49,982
They are packages: code that other people
shared.

14
00:00:50,002 --> 00:00:52,630
Most live on PyPI, at pypi dot org.

15
00:00:52,667 --> 00:00:57,233
Anyone can publish there, so a look-alike
name can hide harmful code.

16
00:00:57,253 --> 00:01:00,624
And her board must run the same way on two
laptops.

17
00:01:01,217 --> 00:01:02,741
Here is the big idea.

18
00:01:02,761 --> 00:01:08,700
A virtual environment is a folder that
holds its own Python and its own packages,

19
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:09,877
for one project.

20
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:14,981
Without one, every project's packages get
mixed together in the computer's Python.

21
00:01:15,587 --> 00:01:18,243
Think of a separate toolbox for each
project.

22
00:01:18,263 --> 00:01:22,467
The weather board's tools never get mixed
up with the quiz app's tools.

23
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,279
The comparison stops working at one point.

24
00:01:25,299 --> 00:01:28,478
A virtual environment is built for one
computer.

25
00:01:28,498 --> 00:01:30,810
You cannot carry it to another one.

26
00:01:30,833 --> 00:01:35,798
On a new computer, you build a new one,
from a list of what the project needs.

27
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:39,950
Pip is the tool that installs packages
from PyPI.

28
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,360
The virtual environment is usually a
folder called dot v e n v.

29
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:50,512
And a pinned version names one exact
version, with two equals signs.

30
00:01:50,532 --> 00:01:54,135
A list of them is called requirements dot
t x t.

31
00:01:54,753 --> 00:01:55,435
Quick check.

32
00:01:55,455 --> 00:01:58,765
Where should Asha install tabulate for the
weather board?

33
00:02:10,147 --> 00:02:10,273
C.

34
00:02:10,293 --> 00:02:13,857
Into the weather board's own virtual
environment.

35
00:02:13,877 --> 00:02:16,271
Each project has its own toolbox.

36
00:02:16,887 --> 00:02:17,463
Breather.

37
00:02:17,483 --> 00:02:20,909
Next, Asha builds the toolbox for her
weather board.

38
00:02:25,650 --> 00:02:26,155
Step 1.

39
00:02:26,175 --> 00:02:29,458
Asha opens a terminal in her project
folder.

40
00:02:29,478 --> 00:02:35,764
She runs v e n v, the standard module that
makes virtual environments, and names the

41
00:02:35,784 --> 00:02:37,190
folder dot v e n v.

42
00:02:37,233 --> 00:02:42,323
On Windows, she types p y, dash m, v e n
v, dot v e n v.

43
00:02:42,367 --> 00:02:46,963
On macOS and Linux, python3, dash m, v e n
v, dot v e n v.

44
00:02:46,983 --> 00:02:52,535
Nothing prints, but a new folder appears,
with its own python and pip.

45
00:02:53,153 --> 00:02:53,661
Step 2.

46
00:02:53,681 --> 00:02:58,783
Activating tells this terminal to use the
Python inside dot v e n v.

47
00:02:58,833 --> 00:03:03,473
On Windows, dot v e n v, backslash,
Scripts, backslash, activate.

48
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:06,383
In PowerShell, the script ends in dot p s
1.

49
00:03:06,403 --> 00:03:09,880
The lesson shows what to do if PowerShell
refuses it.

50
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:15,730
On macOS and Linux, source, dot v e n v,
slash, bin, slash, activate.

51
00:03:15,767 --> 00:03:17,728
To leave it, type deactivate.

52
00:03:17,748 --> 00:03:22,305
And one rule: activate it again every time
you open a new terminal.

53
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:26,583
Now the prompt starts with dot v e n v in
brackets.

54
00:03:26,603 --> 00:03:29,400
Asha checks it with one line of Python.

55
00:03:29,433 --> 00:03:33,344
Sys dot prefix is the folder of the Python
that is running.

56
00:03:33,364 --> 00:03:36,609
Sys dot base prefix is the computer's own
Python.

57
00:03:36,629 --> 00:03:41,073
They differ only inside a virtual
environment, so this prints True.

58
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:46,728
The next morning, Asha opens a new
terminal, and types python straight away.

59
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:00,137
The computer's own Python.

60
00:04:00,157 --> 00:04:05,730
A new terminal starts outside, so she must
activate dot v e n v again.

61
00:04:05,750 --> 00:04:10,603
The prompt tells her: no dot v e n v in
brackets, not inside.

62
00:04:11,183 --> 00:04:11,735
Step 3.

63
00:04:11,755 --> 00:04:15,654
With dot v e n v active, Asha installs
tabulate.

64
00:04:15,674 --> 00:04:19,573
She types python, dash m, pip install,
tabulate.

65
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:24,008
Python dash m makes sure pip installs into
the Python she is running.

66
00:04:24,028 --> 00:04:26,767
Pip list then shows what is installed
here.

67
00:04:26,787 --> 00:04:28,820
Your version numbers may differ.

68
00:04:28,867 --> 00:04:34,160
In Chapter 19, you installed pytest once,
with no virtual environment.

69
00:04:34,180 --> 00:04:39,246
From now on, each project's packages go
inside its own dot v e n v.

70
00:04:39,853 --> 00:04:40,375
Step 4.

71
00:04:40,395 --> 00:04:44,944
Pip freeze prints every installed package
as a pinned line.

72
00:04:44,964 --> 00:04:49,513
The greater than sign saves it into
requirements dot t x t.

73
00:04:49,533 --> 00:04:53,875
At home, Asha creates and activates a new
dot v e n v.

74
00:04:53,895 --> 00:05:00,740
Then pip install, dash r, requirements dot
t x t, installs exactly the same versions.

75
00:05:01,353 --> 00:05:03,109
Here is the board's file.

76
00:05:03,129 --> 00:05:05,383
The hash sign line is a comment.

77
00:05:05,403 --> 00:05:07,727
Each other line pins one package.

78
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:10,527
Asha's program reads that file.

79
00:05:10,547 --> 00:05:14,550
Lines 4 and 5 read the text, and line 6
goes through it.

80
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:18,160
Lines 8 and 9 skip empty lines and
comments.

81
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:23,725
Line 10 splits each line at the two equals
signs, so name gets the package, and

82
00:05:23,745 --> 00:05:25,410
version gets the number.

83
00:05:25,433 --> 00:05:29,493
Line 11 prints the name in ten spaces,
then the version.

84
00:05:40,847 --> 00:05:45,931
Requests and 2.32.3, then tabulate and
0.9.0.

85
00:05:45,951 --> 00:05:50,356
The comment line never reaches line 11.

86
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:51,455
Step 5.

87
00:05:51,475 --> 00:05:55,955
A line without two equals signs lets pip
choose the version.

88
00:05:55,975 --> 00:05:58,580
That version can change next month.

89
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:01,903
Greater than, equals, 8 means 8 or newer.

90
00:06:01,923 --> 00:06:03,930
That is not exact either.

91
00:06:03,967 --> 00:06:08,782
Line 4 calls a line pinned only if it
contains two equals signs together.

92
00:06:20,147 --> 00:06:23,760
Only the first: requests, pinned at
2.32.3.

93
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:28,577
Tabulate has no version, and pytest only
says 8 or newer.

94
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:33,698
Those two lines could install different
code on Asha's two laptops.

95
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:35,102
Quick check.

96
00:06:35,122 --> 00:06:39,313
Which line installs exactly the same
version on every computer?

97
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:50,822
D.

98
00:06:50,842 --> 00:06:53,976
Two equals signs pin one exact version.

99
00:06:53,996 --> 00:06:55,432
Look closely at C.

100
00:06:55,452 --> 00:06:56,726
It has only one.

101
00:06:57,320 --> 00:06:57,944
Breather.

102
00:06:57,964 --> 00:07:02,738
Asha's board now has its own toolbox, and
a list of exact versions.

103
00:07:02,758 --> 00:07:06,601
Next, the most important habit: checking a
name first.

104
00:07:11,350 --> 00:07:11,822
Step 6.

105
00:07:11,842 --> 00:07:17,588
Before Asha installs a package, she opens
its page on pypi dot org, and makes four

106
00:07:17,608 --> 00:07:18,080
checks.

107
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:18,381
One.

108
00:07:18,401 --> 00:07:21,389
The exact name: every letter must match.

109
00:07:21,409 --> 00:07:27,330
PyPI ignores capital letters, so Requests
with a capital R is the same package.

110
00:07:27,367 --> 00:07:30,627
Two, links to its code and documentation.

111
00:07:30,667 --> 00:07:32,877
Three, its release history.

112
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:34,556
Four, who publishes it.

113
00:07:35,153 --> 00:07:39,961
A name one or two letters away from a
famous one is a warning sign.

114
00:07:39,981 --> 00:07:45,293
People publish look-alike names on
purpose, hoping for a spelling mistake.

115
00:07:45,333 --> 00:07:49,847
The standard module diff lib measures how
alike two names are.

116
00:07:49,867 --> 00:07:53,723
Its score goes from 0, nothing alike, to
1, the same.

117
00:07:53,767 --> 00:07:56,719
Line 5 compares reqeusts with requests.

118
00:07:56,739 --> 00:08:01,141
Its first argument, None, means no
characters are ignored.

119
00:08:01,161 --> 00:08:05,867
Even with two letters swapped, line 6
prints a score of 0.875.

120
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:08,271
Line 7 uses get close matches.

121
00:08:08,291 --> 00:08:14,566
It returns a list of the trusted names
whose score is at least the cutoff, here

122
00:08:14,586 --> 00:08:14,885
0.8.

123
00:08:26,247 --> 00:08:33,007
Line 7 prints a list with requests,
because 0.875 is at least 0.8.

124
00:08:33,033 --> 00:08:35,348
Line 8 prints an empty list.

125
00:08:35,368 --> 00:08:38,349
Num py is not like any trusted name.

126
00:08:38,950 --> 00:08:40,449
Back to the mission.

127
00:08:40,469 --> 00:08:46,146
Line 5 is the trusted list, lines 6 and 7
read the draft, and line 8 counts

128
00:08:46,166 --> 00:08:46,830
problems.

129
00:08:46,867 --> 00:08:51,967
Line 13 takes the name before the two
equals signs, in small letters.

130
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,453
Lines 14 to 16 report a line with no pin,
and count it.

131
00:08:57,053 --> 00:09:01,453
Line 17 catches a pinned name that is not
on the trusted list.

132
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:04,554
Line 18 finds the closest trusted names.

133
00:09:04,574 --> 00:09:10,779
Line 19 takes the first one, with the if
else from Chapter 14, in case nothing is

134
00:09:10,799 --> 00:09:11,240
close.

135
00:09:11,267 --> 00:09:14,642
Lines 20 and 21 report it and count it.

136
00:09:14,662 --> 00:09:19,083
Any other line is OK, and line 24 prints
the total.

137
00:09:19,687 --> 00:09:21,342
Here is the draft again.

138
00:09:21,362 --> 00:09:27,135
Use the program's two rules: a line needs
a pin, and a pinned name must be trusted.

139
00:09:38,513 --> 00:09:39,756
Three of the four.

140
00:09:39,776 --> 00:09:44,738
Tabulate and pytest are not pinned, and
reqeusts is not a trusted name.

141
00:09:45,353 --> 00:09:46,267
Asha runs it.

142
00:09:46,287 --> 00:09:51,293
Line 3 of the output shows the look-alike:
unknown, close to requests.

143
00:09:51,333 --> 00:09:52,624
Problems found: 3.

144
00:09:52,644 --> 00:09:55,318
Mission solved: three problems found.

145
00:09:55,338 --> 00:10:00,853
She pins tabulate and pytest, and fixes
the spelling of requests, before she

146
00:10:00,873 --> 00:10:02,163
installs anything.

147
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:07,521
Then she builds her name check into a
function, with an AI assistant, one stage

148
00:10:07,541 --> 00:10:08,197
at a time.

149
00:10:08,783 --> 00:10:13,121
This chapter's AI skill: check every
package an AI suggests.

150
00:10:13,141 --> 00:10:19,078
It can name a package that does not exist,
and attackers can publish exactly those

151
00:10:19,098 --> 00:10:19,513
names.

152
00:10:19,533 --> 00:10:23,328
So never install a name from a chat before
you check it.

153
00:10:23,348 --> 00:10:26,598
And never paste keys, passwords or private
data.

154
00:10:27,220 --> 00:10:28,292
Stage 1: plan.

155
00:10:28,312 --> 00:10:33,204
Asha asks for the steps to add a package
to her project safely.

156
00:10:33,224 --> 00:10:34,140
No code yet.

157
00:10:34,167 --> 00:10:35,331
Four good steps.

158
00:10:35,351 --> 00:10:41,476
But one is missing: check the name and its
page on pypi dot org, before installing.

159
00:10:41,496 --> 00:10:43,179
Asha adds it as step 2.

160
00:10:43,787 --> 00:10:44,876
Stage 2: write.

161
00:10:44,896 --> 00:10:47,613
Asha writes her own name check first.

162
00:10:47,633 --> 00:10:49,537
It only knows exact names.

163
00:10:49,567 --> 00:10:52,149
The AI says reqeusts gives unknown.

164
00:10:52,169 --> 00:10:53,562
She runs it: right.

165
00:10:53,582 --> 00:10:56,982
But her version cannot warn about
look-alikes.

166
00:10:57,587 --> 00:10:58,932
Stage 3: generate.

167
00:10:58,952 --> 00:11:04,547
Asha asks for a version that warns about
look-alike names, using diff lib.

168
00:11:04,567 --> 00:11:09,151
The AI says reqeusts will return:
look-alike of requests.

169
00:11:09,753 --> 00:11:11,094
Look at line 10.

170
00:11:11,114 --> 00:11:12,625
The cutoff is 0.9.

171
00:11:12,645 --> 00:11:16,282
Remember the score of reqeusts from step
6.

172
00:11:27,647 --> 00:11:27,962
No.

173
00:11:27,982 --> 00:11:33,774
Reqeusts scores only 0.875, below the
cutoff of 0.9.

174
00:11:33,794 --> 00:11:36,457
So line 13 says unknown.

175
00:11:37,053 --> 00:11:38,096
Stage 4: test.

176
00:11:38,116 --> 00:11:43,863
Asha writes four cases with the exact
result she expects, including Requests

177
00:11:43,883 --> 00:11:45,153
with a capital R.

178
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:47,769
The AI says all four will pass.

179
00:11:48,387 --> 00:11:51,385
Lines 16 to 19 hold the four cases.

180
00:11:51,405 --> 00:11:54,577
Line 23 prints pass or fail for each.

181
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:58,230
Remember: PyPI ignores capital letters.

182
00:11:58,250 --> 00:11:59,634
Look at line 8.

183
00:12:11,013 --> 00:12:11,650
Two fail.

184
00:12:11,670 --> 00:12:13,986
Reqeusts, because of the cutoff.

185
00:12:14,006 --> 00:12:19,313
And Requests, because line 8 compares the
exact text, with its capital R.

186
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:24,920
Asha shows the AI the two failures, and
asks for the cause of each first.

187
00:12:24,967 --> 00:12:31,247
The AI lowers the cutoff to 0.8, and
changes the name to small letters before

188
00:12:31,267 --> 00:12:32,066
comparing.

189
00:12:32,687 --> 00:12:36,798
Line 8 lowers the name, and line 11 uses
0.8.

190
00:12:36,818 --> 00:12:39,552
She runs all four cases again.

191
00:12:39,572 --> 00:12:40,838
All four pass.

192
00:12:41,453 --> 00:12:42,542
Stage 5: debug.

193
00:12:42,562 --> 00:12:47,198
Asha copies an import line from a chat
into her weather script.

194
00:12:47,218 --> 00:12:50,080
It stops with a module not found error.

195
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:52,223
She asks for the cause first.

196
00:12:52,267 --> 00:12:57,613
The AI says the package is not installed,
and gives her the install command for

197
00:12:57,633 --> 00:12:58,225
reqeusts.

198
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:09,749
No.

199
00:13:09,769 --> 00:13:11,707
Not before she checks the name.

200
00:13:11,727 --> 00:13:14,296
It is the same look-alike from her draft.

201
00:13:14,887 --> 00:13:16,673
She runs her name check.

202
00:13:16,693 --> 00:13:19,157
Reqeusts: look-alike of requests.

203
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:24,777
So the real problem is a spelling mistake
in her import line, not a missing package.

204
00:13:24,797 --> 00:13:30,040
She fixes it to requests, and installs
requests inside her virtual environment.

205
00:13:30,067 --> 00:13:34,124
The look-alike could have run someone
else's code on the cafe laptop.

206
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:37,983
She runs her four cases again on the name
check.

207
00:13:38,003 --> 00:13:39,351
All four still pass.

208
00:13:39,953 --> 00:13:41,253
Stage 6: improve.

209
00:13:41,273 --> 00:13:42,495
Her setup works.

210
00:13:42,515 --> 00:13:45,523
Asha asks for at most two improvements.

211
00:13:45,567 --> 00:13:45,825
One.

212
00:13:45,845 --> 00:13:51,183
Remove spaces with strip, because a name
copied from a chat can carry spaces.

213
00:13:51,203 --> 00:13:52,227
That is useful.

214
00:13:52,267 --> 00:13:52,505
Two.

215
00:13:52,525 --> 00:13:57,598
Skip the virtual environment, and force
the install with break system packages.

216
00:13:57,618 --> 00:14:02,692
That forces pip to change the computer's
own Python, which other programs need.

217
00:14:02,712 --> 00:14:06,109
It breaks this chapter's first rule, so
Asha says no.

218
00:14:06,720 --> 00:14:08,683
She keeps suggestion one.

219
00:14:08,703 --> 00:14:15,427
Line 8 now strips spaces, and line 21 adds
a fifth case: requests, with spaces around

220
00:14:15,447 --> 00:14:15,665
it.

221
00:14:15,685 --> 00:14:17,728
She runs every case again.

222
00:14:17,748 --> 00:14:18,839
All five pass.

223
00:14:19,453 --> 00:14:20,328
Quick check.

224
00:14:20,348 --> 00:14:24,055
An AI says: install this package to fix
the error.

225
00:14:24,075 --> 00:14:25,620
What do you do first?

226
00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:37,122
B.

227
00:14:37,142 --> 00:14:41,172
Check the exact name and its page on pypi
dot org.

228
00:14:41,192 --> 00:14:42,144
Then decide.

229
00:14:42,753 --> 00:14:43,401
Breather.

230
00:14:43,421 --> 00:14:46,072
The AI made four confident mistakes.

231
00:14:46,092 --> 00:14:50,893
A score, test cases, her name check and
one rule caught them all.

232
00:14:50,933 --> 00:14:55,404
Next, four mistakes beginners make with
environments and packages.

233
00:15:00,150 --> 00:15:00,811
Mistake 1.

234
00:15:00,831 --> 00:15:06,052
Asha installs a package in her virtual
environment, opens a new terminal, and

235
00:15:06,072 --> 00:15:07,073
runs her board.

236
00:15:07,093 --> 00:15:10,135
Here, weather tables stands for that
package.

237
00:15:10,155 --> 00:15:12,790
It stops with a module not found error.

238
00:15:12,833 --> 00:15:14,447
Check the prompt first.

239
00:15:14,467 --> 00:15:20,058
With no dot v e n v in brackets, the
terminal uses the computer's Python, where

240
00:15:20,078 --> 00:15:22,118
the package is not installed.

241
00:15:22,138 --> 00:15:23,893
Activate, then run again.

242
00:15:24,487 --> 00:15:25,246
Mistake 2.

243
00:15:25,266 --> 00:15:28,210
Remember the pinned lines from step 5.

244
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:41,593
It has only one equals sign.

245
00:15:41,613 --> 00:15:46,820
So line 3 finds no two equals signs to
split at, and gets only one part.

246
00:15:46,867 --> 00:15:52,553
Not enough values to unpack means: two
names are waiting, but only one value

247
00:15:52,573 --> 00:15:53,153
arrived.

248
00:15:53,173 --> 00:15:54,880
Write two equals signs.

249
00:15:54,900 --> 00:15:57,208
Pip refuses the single one too.

250
00:15:57,820 --> 00:15:58,598
Mistake 3.

251
00:15:58,618 --> 00:16:04,187
Some systems refuse a plain pip install
outside a virtual environment.

252
00:16:04,207 --> 00:16:07,141
Do not force it, as the AI suggested.

253
00:16:07,161 --> 00:16:11,133
Activate a virtual environment, and
install there.

254
00:16:11,753 --> 00:16:12,444
Mistake 4.

255
00:16:12,464 --> 00:16:16,070
A virtual environment holds paths for one
computer.

256
00:16:16,090 --> 00:16:20,193
Copied to another computer or folder, it
may stop working.

257
00:16:20,233 --> 00:16:22,463
So never share dot v e n v.

258
00:16:22,483 --> 00:16:28,713
Share requirements dot t x t, and build a
new environment on each computer.

259
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:30,240
Last breather.

260
00:16:30,260 --> 00:16:33,662
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

261
00:16:38,383 --> 00:16:38,784
First.

262
00:16:38,804 --> 00:16:41,310
A new version of tabulate comes out.

263
00:16:41,330 --> 00:16:45,238
How could Asha try it, without breaking
the cafe laptop?

264
00:16:56,613 --> 00:16:59,046
Keep your answer for the practice tasks.

265
00:16:59,653 --> 00:17:00,100
Second.

266
00:17:00,120 --> 00:17:03,435
An AI suggests a package called cafe
weather easy.

267
00:17:03,455 --> 00:17:08,636
Which checks would you make on its page,
and which result would make you stop?

268
00:17:20,013 --> 00:17:21,224
Write your list down.

269
00:17:21,244 --> 00:17:23,862
You will use it every time you add a
package.

270
00:17:24,450 --> 00:17:29,800
A virtual environment is a project folder
with its own Python and packages.

271
00:17:29,833 --> 00:17:35,073
Look for dot v e n v in the prompt, then
install with python dash m pip.

272
00:17:35,100 --> 00:17:40,260
Requirements dot t x t pins exact versions
for every computer.

273
00:17:40,300 --> 00:17:45,185
And check every name before you install
it, especially a name from an AI.

274
00:17:45,787 --> 00:17:46,822
One last check.

275
00:17:46,842 --> 00:17:52,311
Which command records the exact versions
in the active environment, in a file?

276
00:18:03,680 --> 00:18:03,810
A.

277
00:18:03,830 --> 00:18:09,143
Pip freeze prints pinned lines, and the
greater than sign saves them in

278
00:18:09,163 --> 00:18:10,870
requirements dot t x t.

279
00:18:10,890 --> 00:18:12,748
Pip list only shows them.

280
00:18:13,353 --> 00:18:14,650
Mission complete!

281
00:18:14,670 --> 00:18:17,903
You earned 100 XP and the Toolsmith badge.

282
00:18:20,150 --> 00:18:21,826
Now it is your turn.

283
00:18:21,846 --> 00:18:24,031
Eight tasks, worth 170 XP.

284
00:18:24,051 --> 00:18:26,660
None of them installs anything.

285
00:18:26,700 --> 00:18:29,880
Three warm-up tasks, worth 10 XP each.

286
00:18:29,900 --> 00:18:32,270
Three apply tasks, worth 20 each.

287
00:18:32,300 --> 00:18:34,964
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

288
00:18:34,984 --> 00:18:38,229
In the last one, you fix an AI's pin
checker.

289
00:18:38,817 --> 00:18:44,359
Asha's quiz scores sit in a CSV file, her
weather board saves JSON, and her loaders

290
00:18:44,379 --> 00:18:45,431
handle bad rows.

291
00:18:45,451 --> 00:18:49,117
But her day's numbers are still scattered
across files.

292
00:18:49,167 --> 00:18:54,636
In Chapter 25, Boss Level 5, you will
build the Data Report Generator: a tool

293
00:18:54,656 --> 00:18:59,627
that reads a data file, handles bad rows,
and writes one clear report.

294
00:18:59,667 --> 00:19:02,157
Do the warm-up tasks now.

295
00:19:02,200 --> 00:19:03,851
See you at the boss level.
